title: Changing keybindings
tags: Keybindings Configuration
kind: guide
aliases: rebind keybindings.toml keybinding-editor settings
summary: Bind, rebind or unbind a key from the config panel or by editing gui/keybindings.toml, without restarting.

To find out what a key does now, press <<key "help:key" "h k">> then the key
([[In-app help]]). The command input also shows, next to each command, the keys
that run it.

!! From the GUI

`panel:open here config` (<<key "panel:open here config" "t c">>) opens the
[[config panel]]. It lists every binding in effect: the key, the invocation, and
where it applies — `global`, a panel type, or `@name` for a text
field — followed by `⌨` when it also applies while typing ([[Keybindings]]).

* ''Look up'' a key or a command: type it in the form. The table narrows to the keys it starts and to the bindings of that command, so whether a key is free shows before anything is saved.
* ''Add'': fill in the form — key, command, and optionally `when`, `focus` and `text-input` — and press ''Save'' (or Enter).
* ''Change'': ''Edit'' on a row copies it into the form; ''Save'' replaces that binding, wherever the form now puts it. Moving a command to another key is one save.
* ''Unbind'': ''Remove'' on the row.

A change applies at once, in every panel, and is written to the file.

!!! What a save refuses

The GUI checks a binding before writing it, and the panel shows the same check as
the form is typed, field by field; ''Save'' waits until nothing stands in the way.

* ''A command that does not exist.'' Only the first word is a name — a binding may pre-fill arguments (`panel:open here file`) — and a shell line (`!…`) names none. The commands known are those the command input lists: the shell's, every panel's, and `commands.js`'s.
* ''A key already bound in the same place'' (same `when`, same `focus`) — to change what it runs, ''Edit'' its row. The same key elsewhere is no conflict: the most specific place wins where both apply.
* ''A key that starts a sequence bound in the same place, or a sequence one of whose first keys is bound there alone'': a key alone runs before any sequence it starts, so one of the two could never run. `g` cannot join `g g` in one place.

A key suggested by a panel or by `commands.js` is listed too, marked ''suggested'',
but is not in the file: there is nothing to remove, and the suggestion would be made
again at the next start. It has no ''Remove''. ''Edit'' it and save it in the same
place to override it — the file wins over a suggestion; moving it is refused, since
the panel would suggest it again.

!! In the file

`~/.config/metafolder/gui/keybindings.toml` has one entry per key. Its value is one
binding, or a list of them when the key does different things in different places:

```
"ctrl+t" = { command = "workspace:new" }
"ctrl+a" = { command = "editing:goto line-start", text-input = true }

"shift+r" = [
  { command = "log:revert", when = "log" },
  { command = "file:restart", when = "file" },
]

"enter" = [
  { command = "editing:confirm", text-input = true },
  { command = "find:next", focus = "find" },
]
```

A key appears once in the file, which is why its bindings are a list, and has one
binding per place: the same key bound twice with the same `when` and `focus` is
refused, also when it is written in two ways (`"shift+ctrl+a"` and `"ctrl+shift+a"`
are one key).

After editing the file, `config:reload keybindings` reads it again. A file that
cannot be read as TOML, a malformed key or a key bound twice in the same place is
refused with the reason, and the
bindings in effect stay. At startup the same mistake stops the GUI
([[No runtime fallback]]).

The config panel edits this same file, and only the entry concerned: comments,
order and layout are kept. That matters beyond tidiness — an update of metafolder
merges the newly shipped keys into this file ([[Updating the configuration]]), and a
file rewritten from top to bottom would conflict with every one of them.

!! Going back to the shipped keys

The file is the whole set, the shipped keys and yours together; there is no
separate list of defaults that an entry could fall back to. Unbinding a key leaves
it unbound. Getting a shipped binding back is done with git
([[The keybindings file]]).

!! A key that does nothing

A binding whose command does not exist runs nothing. At startup the GUI lists such
bindings in the message log ([[Commands]]) — a typo, or a command that was renamed.
